    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "If you're worried about the delivery, you can soften the language by framing it as a shared goal for team optimization rather than a personal failing. You could frame it as an opportunity for them to maximize their impact on the *team's* flow, rather than focusing strictly on the lateness itself.\n\nFor example, instead of stating it as a required correction, you could phrase it as an area where alignment could be even stronger. You might say something like, \"To further amplify their impact across the entire department, focusing on consistent presence at our morning stand-ups will help ensure their valuable insights are incorporated right from the start of our daily planning.\" This shifts the focus from \"you are late\" to \"we benefit when you are present.\"",
